Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑run solution** that does exactly what you asked for:

| ✅ What it does |
|---|
| 1️⃣ Finds every file that lives in a specific Google Drive folder (by folder ID). |
| 2️⃣ Pulls the **file name** and the **shareable URL** for each file. |
| 3️⃣ Writes those two pieces of data into a Google Sheet – one row per file, name in column A, URL in column B. |

The whole thing is built with **Google Apps Script**, which runs inside the Sheet you’ll use as the destination. No external services, no extra libraries – just a few lines of JavaScript and a couple of clicks.

---

## 1️⃣ Set‑up the destination Google Sheet

1. Open (or create) the Google Sheet where you want the list to appear.  
   *Tip:* Give the sheet a clear name like **“Drive‑Folder Index”**.

2. In the sheet, click **Extensions → Apps Script**.  
   This opens the Apps Script editor attached to that spreadsheet.

---

## 2️⃣ Paste the script

Replace any starter code with the script below.  
(If you already have other functions, just add the new ones – they won’t interfere.)

```javascript
/**
 * MAIN ENTRY POINT
 *
 * 1️⃣ Put your Drive folder ID in the constant FOLDER_ID.
 * 2️⃣ (Optional) Put a different sheet name in SHEET_NAME if you aren't using the first sheet.
 * 3️⃣ Run listFolderFiles() – the script will fill the sheet with Name / URL pairs.
 */
function listFolderFiles() {
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 👉 1️⃣ EDIT THESE TWO VALUES BEFORE RUNNING
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const FOLDER_ID = 'YOUR_FOLDER_ID_HERE';   // <-- replace with the ID of the folder you want to scan
  const SHEET_NAME = 'Sheet1';               // <-- name of the sheet/tab where data will be written
  // --------------------------------------------------------------

  // Grab the folder (throws an error if the ID is wrong)
  const folder = DriveApp.getFolderById(FOLDER_ID);

  // Get the sheet (creates it if it doesn't exist)
  const ss = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet();
  let sheet = ss.getSheetByName(SHEET_NAME);
  if (!sheet) {
    sheet = ss.insertSheet(SHEET_NAME);
  }

  // OPTIONAL: clear previous data (keep header row)
  sheet.clearContents();
  sheet.appendRow(['File Name', 'File URL']);   // header

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Iterate through every file in the folder
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const files = folder.getFiles();   // returns a FileIterator
  const rows = [];                   // we’ll batch‑write at the end (faster)

  while (files.hasNext()) {
    const file = files.next();

    // Get the name
    const name = file.getName();

    // Build a URL that opens the file in the browser.
    // For most file types this works: https://drive.google.com/file/d/FILE_ID/view
    const url = `https://drive.google.com/file/d/${file.getId()}/view`;

    rows.push([name, url]);
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Write everything to the sheet in one go (fast!)
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  if (rows.length) {
    sheet.getRange(2, 1, rows.length, 2).setValues(rows);
  }

  // Optional: auto‑size columns for readability
  sheet.autoResizeColumns(1, 2);
}
```

### What you need to edit

| Variable | Where to find it | What to paste |
|---|---|---|
| `FOLDER_ID` | Open the Drive folder → look at the URL. It looks like `https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/**1a2b3cD4e5F6g7H8i9J0kL**`. The bold part is the ID. | Replace `YOUR_FOLDER_ID_HERE` with that string (no quotes inside). |
| `SHEET_NAME` *(optional)* | The name of the tab where you want the data (default is “Sheet1”). | Change if you prefer another tab name. |

---

## 3️⃣ Authorize & run

1. Click the **Save** (💾) button.  
2. Click the **Run** ▶️ button next to `listFolderFiles`.  
3. The first time you run it you’ll see an **Authorization** dialog – grant the script permission to read your Drive and edit the Sheet.  
4. After the script finishes, switch back to your spreadsheet. You should see a header row and then one row per file: **File Name** | **File URL**.

---

## 4️⃣ (Optional) Keep the list up‑to‑date automatically

If you want the sheet to refresh whenever the folder changes, you can add a **time‑driven trigger** (e.g., every hour) or a **simple on‑edit trigger** that you manually invoke.

### Add a daily trigger (example)

```javascript
function createDailyTrigger() {
  // Deletes any existing trigger for this function (prevents duplicates)
  const allTriggers = ScriptApp.getProjectTriggers();
  for (const t of allTriggers) {
    if (t.getHandlerFunction() === 'listFolderFiles') {
      ScriptApp.deleteTrigger(t);
    }
  }
  // Create a new trigger that runs once a day at 2 am (adjust as needed)
  ScriptApp.newTrigger('listFolderFiles')
           .timeBased()
           .atHour(2)
           .everyDays(1)
           .create();
}
```

Run `createDailyTrigger()` once, and the script will automatically rebuild the list each day.

---

## 5️⃣ What if you have **thousands** of files?

The `folder.getFiles()` iterator works fine for many files, but Apps Script has a **6‑minute execution limit** per run. If you suspect you’ll exceed that (e.g., > 10 k files), you can:

| Approach | How it works |
|---|---|
| **Chunked processing** | Store the last processed file’s ID in the sheet (or in `PropertiesService`) and resume from there on the next trigger run. |
| **Drive API pagination** | Use the advanced Drive service (`Drive.Files.list`) with `pageSize` and `pageToken`. This gives you control over batch size and lets you continue across multiple executions. |
| **Export via Google Takeout** | For a one‑off massive export, use Google Takeout to get a CSV of your Drive metadata. |

For most everyday use‑cases (a few hundred to a few thousand files) the simple iterator above is more than enough.

---

## 6️⃣ TL;DR – One‑click checklist

| Step | Action |
|---|---|
| 1️⃣ | Open the destination Google Sheet → **Extensions → Apps Script** |
| 2️⃣ | Paste the script (replace `YOUR_FOLDER_ID_HERE` with your folder’s ID) |
| 3️⃣ | Click **Run** → authorize the script |
| 4️⃣ | Return to the sheet – you’ll see the file list |
| 5️⃣ *(optional)* | Run `createDailyTrigger()` once to keep the list refreshed automatically |

That’s it! 🎉 You now have a live, maintainable index of every file in any Drive folder, right inside a Google Sheet. Feel free to tweak the script (add more columns, filter by MIME type, etc.) – the core pattern stays the same. Happy automating!

cloudient-personal-digital-assistant-1:
Below are a few simple ways to pull **all table names** from a MySQL database with PHP.  
Pick the style (mysqli procedural, mysqli OO, or PDO) that matches the rest of your code.

---

## 1. Using `SHOW TABLES` (the most straightforward query)

### a) MySQLi – procedural

```php
<?php
$host = 'localhost';
$user = 'my_user';
$pass = 'my_pass';
$db   = 'my_database';

$link = mysqli_connect($host, $user, $pass, $db);
if (!$link) {
    die('Connect error: ' . mysqli_connect_error());
}

/* Run the query */
$res = mysqli_query($link, 'SHOW TABLES');
if (!$res) {
    die('Query error: ' . mysqli_error($link));
}

/* Fetch the table names */
$tables = [];
while ($row = mysqli_fetch_row($res)) {
    $tables[] = $row[0];          // each row contains a single column: the table name
}

/* Example output */
print_r($tables);
?>
```

### b) MySQLi – OO

```php
<?php
$mysqli = new mysqli('localhost', 'my_user', 'my_pass', 'my_database');
if ($mysqli->connect_errno) {
    die('Connect error: ' . $mysqli->connect_error);
}

$result = $mysqli->query('SHOW TABLES');
$tables = $result->fetch_all(MYSQLI_NUM);   // returns an array of rows
$tables = array_column($tables, 0);         // flatten to a simple list

print_r($tables);
?>
```

### c) PDO

```php
<?php
$dsn  = 'mysql:host=localhost;dbname=my_database;charset=utf8';
$pdo  = new PDO($dsn, 'my_user', 'my_pass', [
    PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION,
]);

$stmt   = $pdo->query('SHOW TABLES');
$tables = $stmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_COLUMN, 0);   // column 0 = table name

print_r($tables);
?>
```

---

## 2. Using the `information_schema` (useful if you need extra metadata)

```php
<?php
$pdo = new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=my_database;charset=utf8',
               'my_user', 'my_pass',
               [PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION]);

$sql = "
    SELECT TABLE_NAME
    FROM information_schema.TABLES
    WHERE TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE()
    ORDER BY TABLE_NAME
";

$stmt   = $pdo->query($sql);
$tables = $stmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_COLUMN);

print_r($tables);
?>
```

*`DATABASE()`* returns the name of the currently selected database, so you don’t have to hard‑code it.

---

## 3. Quick helper function (PDO)

```php
function getAllTables(PDO $pdo): array
{
    $stmt = $pdo->query('SHOW TABLES');
    return $stmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_COLUMN);
}

/* Usage */
$pdo = new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=my_database;charset=utf8',
               'my_user', 'my_pass',
               [PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION]);

$tables = getAllTables($pdo);
print_r($tables);
```

---

### Things to keep in mind

| Issue | What to do |
|-------|------------|
| **Connection errors** | Always check the return value of `mysqli_connect()` / `new PDO()` and handle `mysqli_connect_error()` or catch `PDOException`. |
| **Permissions** | The MySQL user must have at least `SHOW VIEW` (or `SELECT` on `information_schema`) to see the table list. |
| **Character set** | Set the connection charset (`charset=utf8` in DSN or `mysqli_set_charset($link, 'utf8')`) to avoid garbled names. |
| **Filtering** | If you only need tables that match a pattern, add `LIKE 'prefix_%'` to the `SHOW TABLES` query. |
| **Performance** | For a single database the `SHOW TABLES` query is cheap; using `information_schema` adds a tiny overhead but gives you extra columns (engine, creation time, etc.) if you need them. |

That’s it—run one of the snippets above and you’ll have an array of every table name in the selected MySQL database.

radaroo-1:
Below is a **complete, minimal‑working example** that shows how you can:

1. **Log a user in with Twitter** (OAuth 1.0a).  
2. **Pull their profile + recent tweets**.  
3. **Extract simple “interests”** (hashtags + most‑common words).  
4. **Store the data** in MongoDB.  
5. **Match users** by overlapping interests (Jaccard similarity).  

Everything is kept short and easy to read – you can copy‑paste the files, run `npm install`, and you’ll have a tiny dating‑style service that matches people based on what they tweet about.

---

## 1. Project layout

```
dating‑app/
│
├─ .env                # your secrets (see below)
├─ package.json
├─ server.js           # Express server, routes, session
├─ auth.js             # Twitter OAuth helpers
├─ models/
│   └─ User.js         # Mongoose schema
├─ utils/
│   ├─ interestExtractor.js   # turn tweets → set of interests
│   └─ matcher.js              # compute similarity & find matches
└─ public/
    └─ index.html      # tiny front‑end (login + matches)
```

---

## 2. Install dependencies

```bash
npm init -y
npm i express express-session dotenv mongoose twitter-api-v2 cors
npm i -D nodemon   # optional, for dev
```

Add a simple `package.json` script:

```json
{
  "name": "dating-app",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "main": "server.js",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node server.js",
    "dev": "nodemon server.js"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "cors": "^2.8.5",
    "dotenv": "^16.0.0",
    "express": "^4.18.2",
    "express-session": "^1.17.3",
    "mongoose": "^7.0.0",
    "twitter-api-v2": "^1.15.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "nodemon": "^3.0.0"
  }
}
```

---

## 3. Environment variables (`.env`)

```
PORT=3000
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017/datingapp
SESSION_SECRET=super‑secret‑string

TWITTER_CONSUMER_KEY=YOUR_TWITTER_API_KEY
TWITTER_CONSUMER_SECRET=YOUR_TWITTER_API_SECRET
TWITTER_CALLBACK_URL=http://localhost:3000/auth/twitter/callback
```

> **Tip:** Create a free Twitter developer app → “OAuth 1.0a” keys.  
> The callback URL must match what you set in the dev portal.

---

## 4. Mongoose user model (`models/User.js`)

```js
// models/User.js
const mongoose = require('mongoose');

const UserSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  twitterId: { type: String, required: true, unique: true },
  username: String,
  name: String,
  avatarUrl: String,
  interests: [String],          // e.g. ['#music', 'coffee', 'travel']
});

module.exports = mongoose.model('User', UserSchema);
```

---

## 5. Interest extraction (`utils/interestExtractor.js`)

A *very* simple extractor – it pulls:

* All hashtags from the tweet text.
* The top 5 most frequent non‑stop‑words (≥3 letters).

```js
// utils/interestExtractor.js
const STOP_WORDS = new Set([
  'the','and','for','you','that','with','this','have','from','your',
  'just','like','but','not','are','was','what','when','who','how',
  // add more if you like
]);

function extractInterests(tweets) {
  const wordFreq = {};
  const interests = new Set();

  tweets.forEach(t => {
    // 1️⃣ hashtags
    const hashtags = t.text.match(/#\w+/g);
    if (hashtags) hashtags.forEach(h => interests.add(h.toLowerCase()));

    // 2️⃣ words
    const words = t.text
      .replace(/[^\w\s]/g, ' ')   // strip punctuation
      .toLowerCase()
      .split(/\s+/)
      .filter(w => w.length >= 3 && !STOP_WORDS.has(w));

    words.forEach(w => {
      wordFreq[w] = (wordFreq[w] || 0) + 1;
    });
  });

  // keep top‑5 frequent words as interests
  const topWords = Object.entries(wordFreq)
    .sort((a,b) => b[1] - a[1])
    .slice(0,5)
    .map(e => e[0]);

  topWords.forEach(w => interests.add(w));

  return Array.from(interests);
}

module.exports = { extractInterests };
```

> **Why this simple approach?**  
> It needs no external NLP library, runs instantly, and still gives a decent “interest” signal for a demo.

---

## 6. Matching logic (`utils/matcher.js`)

We compute **Jaccard similarity** between two interest sets:

```
similarity = |A ∩ B| / |A ∪ B|
```

If the similarity is ≥ 0.3 we consider the users a match.

```js
// utils/matcher.js
function jaccard(setA, setB) {
  const a = new Set(setA);
  const b = new Set(setB);
  const intersection = new Set([...a].filter(x => b.has(x)));
  const union = new Set([...a, ...b]);
  return intersection.size / union.size;
}

// Return an array of {userId, username, similarity}
async function findMatches(currentUser, allUsers, threshold = 0.3) {
  const matches = [];

  for (const u of allUsers) {
    if (u.id === currentUser.id) continue; // skip self
    const sim = jaccard(currentUser.interests, u.interests);
    if (sim >= threshold) {
      matches.push({
        userId: u.id,
        username: u.username,
        name: u.name,
        avatarUrl: u.avatarUrl,
        similarity: sim.toFixed(2),
      });
    }
  }

  // sort most similar first
  matches.sort((a,b) => b.similarity - a.similarity);
  return matches;
}

module.exports = { findMatches };
```

---

## 7. Twitter OAuth helpers (`auth.js`)

We use **`twitter-api-v2`** which wraps the OAuth flow nicely.

```js
// auth.js
const { TwitterApi } = require('twitter-api-v2');
const User = require('./models/User');
const { extractInterests } = require('./utils/interestExtractor');

const client = new TwitterApi({
  appKey:    process.env.TWITTER_CONSUMER_KEY,
  appSecret: process.env.TWITTER_CONSUMER_SECRET,
});

async function getRequestToken(req, res) {
  try {
    const { url, oauth_token, oauth_token_secret } = await client.generateAuthLink(
      process.env.TWITTER_CALLBACK_URL,
      { authAccessType: 'read' }
    );

    // store secret in session for later verification
    req.session.oauth_token_secret = oauth_token_secret;
    res.redirect(url);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Twitter request token error', e);
    res.status(500).send('Twitter auth failed');
  }
}

async function handleCallback(req, res) {
  const { oauth_token, oauth_verifier } = req.query;
  const oauth_token_secret = req.session.oauth_token_secret;

  if (!oauth_token || !oauth_verifier || !oauth_token_secret) {
    return res.status(400).send('Missing OAuth data');
  }

  try {
    const { client: loggedClient, accessToken, accessSecret, screenName, userId } =
      await client.login(oauth_verifier, oauth_token, oauth_token_secret);

    // 1️⃣ fetch profile
    const profile = await loggedClient.v2.me({
      'user.fields': ['profile_image_url', 'name', 'username'],
    });

    // 2️⃣ fetch recent tweets (max 50)
    const tweetsResp = await loggedClient.v2.userTimeline(userId, {
      max_results: 50,
      exclude: ['replies', 'retweets'],
    });
    const tweets = tweetsResp.data?.data || [];

    // 3️⃣ extract interests
    const interests = extractInterests(tweets);

    // 4️⃣ upsert user in DB
    const user = await User.findOneAndUpdate(
      { twitterId: userId },
      {
        twitterId: userId,
        username: profile.data.username,
        name: profile.data.name,
        avatarUrl: profile.data.profile_image_url,
        interests,
      },
      { upsert: true, new: true }
    );

    // store minimal session data
    req.session.userId = user.id;
    res.redirect('/');
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Twitter callback error', e);
    res.status(500).send('Twitter auth failed');
  }
}

module.exports = { getRequestToken, handleCallback };
```

